; Anvil - a bare-metal serial monitor for the Raspberry Pi 4,
; written in PureMetal Forge. Version 2.0.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT IT REPLACES
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; Everything anybody on this bench still opens U-Boot for:
;
;   a console            -> banner, prompt, line editor
;   receive over serial  -> b   (raw block, OUR protocol - the fast path)
;                           l   (Motorola S-records - the proven path)
;   jump to it           -> g <addr>
;   report the result    -> "## returned, x0 = ..."   after g
;   look at memory       -> m <addr> <count>         (U-Boot's md, and
;                           md.b / md.w / md.l group the same bytes as
;                           8/16/32-bit values - the count stays bytes)
;   change memory        -> w <addr> <value>
;   fill a range         -> fill <addr> <value> [n]  (U-Boot's mw)
;   copy a range         -> copy <src> <dst> <n>     (U-Boot's cp)
;   compare two ranges   -> compare <a> <b> <n>      (U-Boot's cmp)
;   checksum a range     -> crc32 <addr> <n>
;   a crypto checksum    -> sha1sum / sha256sum <addr> <n>
;   work out a value     -> setexpr <name> <v1> <op> <v2>   (into settings)
;   a default address    -> base [addr]
;   settings that stay   -> settings / wifi, AND the U-Boot names over
;                           the same store: printenv, setenv <n> <v>,
;                           saveenv, and env print/set/save/delete
;   say something         -> echo [-n] <args>        (U-Boot's echo)
;   wait a bit            -> sleep <seconds>          (interruptible)
;   what version is this  -> version                  (U-Boot's version)
;   fetch it over the    -> get <name> [addr]   (U-Boot's tftpboot)
;   network              -> put <name> <addr> <len>  (tftpput)
;                        -> net                  (its ipaddr, serverip,
;                                                 netmask and gatewayip)
;   what is this board   -> i        revision, temperature, every clock
;   how fast is it       -> c [hz]   show or set the ARM clock
;   run it on its own    -> a <addr> + the boot-delay banner (autoboot)
;
; THE BUILD LINE, AND IT IS NOW A PLAIN ONE:
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4/Board/board.pi4 -t pi4
;       -o RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.img
;
; It used to carry --load-addr 0x200000 --bss-addr 0x8200000 and it no
; longer needs to: this file DECLARES its own placement, further down,
; with LoadAddress / BssAddress / StackAddress. See WHERE THIS IMAGE RUNS
; below. The flags still exist and still work; passing one that
; DISAGREES with the source stops the build and names both values. The
; reason for the change is the IDE, which embeds the compiler and cannot
; pass a flag at all - so this program could not be built correctly from
; it. Building it from the IDE and building it from a script now produce
; the same bytes, and that was checked by producing both and comparing
; them.
;
; THE SOURCE IS board.pi4 AND THE OUTPUT IS STILL anvil.img. This file is
; the sole Pi 4 compile target; the old RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.pi4 -
; a byte-identical monolith mirror of this one - was RETIRED on
; 2026-08-31. The output name is unchanged on purpose: the flash and
; console tools default to RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.img (tools/anvil.py,
; anvil_update.py, anvil_wifi_update.py, monsession.py), so building here
; with -o that path keeps every one of them working with no argument.
;
; --bss-addr IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IS NOT A TUNING KNOB. It is what puts
; this program's variables outside the two-megabyte window its code has
; to live in, and #MON_DATA_LO further down is the same number written
; where the running program can see it. Build without it and the two
; disagree: BSS goes back to $00280000, HitsMonitor() still refuses
; writes to #MON_DATA_LO where nothing lives, and nothing refuses a write to
; the variables that are now back inside the image window. See THE
; CEILING AND WHERE IT WENT below, and the gate that checks the two
; numbers against each other, tools/a64/a64_anvil_check.py.
;
; THIS IS kernel8.img. The header used to say "U-Boot STAYS UNDERNEATH
; for now. This is NOT kernel8.img", with a paragraph about Ctrl-C
; putting a working loader back in front of you. U-Boot is GONE from the
; stick - Lib/safety.pi4:362-365 says so and says what it costs: there is
; no recovery path on this board that does not involve physical access.
; The firmware loads this image whole, off a USB stick, to $200000 -
; Lib/mmu.pi4:84, "Anvil is loaded by the firmware as kernel8.img at
; $200000" - and nothing else runs first. pi4Install.pi4 is what writes
; the stick, and its CONFIG.TXT is the thing that names the address.
;
; =====================================================================
;  2.0 - THIS FILE STOPPED OWNING THE HARDWARE - 2026-08-25
; =====================================================================
; Version 1.x hand-rolled a PL011 driver, an architectural-counter read,
; a decimal printer and a set of BCM2711 register constants, because
; when it was written none of those existed anywhere else. All four now
; exist as libraries that are verified against the same silicon, and
; every one of them has been DELETED from this file:
;
;   PutC/PutS/NL/GetC     -> Lib/uart.pi4    UartWrite / UartWriteStr /
;                            PrintNl / UartRead, plus the Print router
;                            so Print("...") and Print(n) work directly
;   #UART_DR #UART_FR     -> Lib/uart.pi4 owns the register map. Two
;   #FR_TXFF #FR_RXFE        spellings of one address is how they drift.
;   Ticks() TickHz()      -> Lib/timer.pi4  (same mrs, one copy)
;   PutDec()              -> Lib/uart.pi4   PrintDec()
;   the 2-second timeout  -> still ours, but built on Lib/timer.pi4's
;                            counter rather than a private one
;
; THE POINT IS NOT THE LINE COUNT, though it went down. It is that a
; place where the monitor and a library disagreed about a register was a
; bug waiting to be found on the bench, and there is now no such place.
; The routing trap, the baud divisor, the mini-UART's opposite bit
; senses, CNTFRQ_EL0's provenance: all of that is documented ONCE, in
; uart.pi4 and timer.pi4, and this header no longer restates it.
;
; READ Lib/uart.pi4's HEADER BEFORE DEBUGGING SILENCE. Short version:
; the PL011 only reaches header pins 8 and 10 with dtoverlay=disable-bt,
; and writing to the UART that is not on the wire SUCCEEDS. Nothing
; fails; nothing arrives.
;
; UartInit(), NOT UartAttach() - CHANGED when this became bootable.
;
; The old comment here said adoption was right because we arrive by "go"
; from a loader that has already set up the port, and that "the day this
; becomes kernel8.img, that one call changes". This is that day.
;
; Booted by the firmware there is no loader and nothing has touched the
; PL011: no pin mux, no pulls, no divisor. Adopting an unconfigured port
; writes no register and produces no output, and produces it silently -
; the same non-failure the header warns about further down.
;
; UartInit is correct in BOTH cases. It muxes GPIO 14/15, sets the
; pulls, and programs the divisor from scratch, so it does not care what
; state the port was in. Under U-Boot it briefly disables a working port
; and reprograms it to the same 115200 8N1; the cost is whatever U-Boot
; had queued, which by this point is nothing.
;
; WHAT IS STILL HAND-WRITTEN HERE, AND WHY IT HAS TO BE:
;
;   PutHexN and friends   There is no 64-bit hex printer in any Pi 4
;                         library. string.pi4's Hex() formats a .w - a
;                         SIXTEEN-bit value - into a buffer, which is a
;                         different job, and DisplayPrintHex() writes to
;                         glass. Sixteen hex digits of x0 is the most
;                         valuable line this program prints and it is
;                         not going through a 16-bit formatter.
;   CacheLine /           No library does I-cache maintenance, and none
;   CacheFlushRange       should: display.pi4 uses "dc cvac" because its
;                         reader is the display controller, which is the
;                         DMA case. Ours is the self-modifying-code case
;                         and wants "dc cvau" + "ic ivau". Same family,
;                         different instruction, opposite mistake.
;   CallAddr              A loader jumping into a payload and surviving
;                         the return is this program's entire reason to
;                         exist. Nothing generic can own it.
;   Crc32                 Nothing else on this part needs one yet.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE MEMORY MAP THIS MONITOR IMPOSES, AND WHY IT HAS TWO WINDOWS
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; UNCHANGED IN 2.0 except for 256 bytes named below. If you are checking
; whether a payload built for an older monitor still loads: it does.
;
; U-Boot's own bdinfo on THIS board (measured, not from a web page)
; reports DRAM in two banks:
;
;     bank 0   $00000000 .. $3B2FFFFF        ~944 MiB
;     bank 1   $40000000 .. $FBFFFFFF        ~3 GiB
;
; and U-Boot relocates itself to roughly $3B03C000, reserving upward
; from there. The second bank was UNREACHABLE under the 32-bit model -
; not because 3 GiB does not fit in 32 bits (it does; $FBFFFFFF is under
; 4 GiB) but because every address the monitor compared was a SIGNED
; .i, so anything from $80000000 up was negative and every range test
; rejected it. Three quarters of the board's DRAM was invisible for a
; reason that was entirely about the type.
;
;   $00000000..$00000FFF  firmware spin table and the secondary cores'
;                         release mailboxes           DO NOT TOUCH
;   $00001000..$000FFFFF  monitor stack, growing DOWN from $00100000
;   $00100000             #MON_STACK  (monitor stack top)
;   $001FF000..$001FFFFF  #AB_BASE - the autoboot record. MOVED HERE ON
;                         2026-09-08, out of the image window, because
;                         that window stopped having a top. A fixed page
;                         BELOW the load address: no image size can
;                         reach an address under its own base. See
;                         AUTOBOOT below for why it cannot be a Global,
;                         and THE MEMORY MAP in Board/memmap.pi4 for the
;                         four properties that chose this page.
;   $00200000..                   monitor image - CODE ONLY since
;                         2026-08-28, and SINCE 2026-09-08 IT HAS NO
;                         TOP. There is no second number on this line
;                         because there is no constant: the image ends
;                         where the image ends (__image_end__), the
;                         monitor reserves the megabyte it ends in, and
;                         `map` at the prompt prints both. Today's build
;                         is 2,106,560 bytes and ends at $004024BF, so
;                         today it reserves $00200000..$004FFFFF and the
;                         low window starts at $00500000. THOSE ARE NOT
;                         THE NUMBERS THE CONSTANTS USED TO CARRY, and
;                         they stopped being them on the day the DSI
;                         work merged - which is the whole argument for
;                         deriving them. See THE CEILING AND WHERE IT
;                         WENT below.
;   ...      ..$07EFFFFF  the LOW payload window, #PAY0_HI at the top and
;                         its bottom edge computed - HwPayLo(0). 123 MiB
;                         with today's monitor
;   $07F00000..$080FFFFF  the payload's own stack lives here - see below
;   $08100000..$0817FFFF  #ETH_RX_BASE - the Ethernet receive ring, half
;                         a megabyte of it. NEW, and it is inside the band
;                         below that was already never offered to a
;                         payload, so no window moved to make room. See
;                         THE ETHERNET RECEIVE REGION under THE NETWORK.
;   $08200000..$0BFFFFFF  named DSI framebuffer, module arena, V3D arena
;                         and screenshot reservations (see memmap.pi4)
;   $0C000000..$0CFFFFFF  monitor Vulkan device-memory heap
;   $0D000000..$0DFFFFFF  #MON_DATA_LO..#MON_DATA_HI - 16 MiB monitor BSS
;                         reservation, checked against the linker map.
;                         It follows the Vulkan heap and is protected by
;                         HitsMonitor().
;   $0E000000..$3B2FFFFF  free, but DELIBERATELY NOT OFFERED - see below
;   $3B03C000..$3B2FFFFF  U-Boot relocated, its heap, its stack, and the
;                         code the three parked cores are spinning in.
;                         HISTORICAL, and left in the map on purpose:
;                         U-Boot is no longer on the stick (safety.pi4:
;                         362-365), so nothing of it is up there now and
;                         the parked cores are in the FIRMWARE's spin
;                         table at $00000000..$00000FFF instead
;                         (smp.pi4:361-364 names the four release
;                         words). The line stays because the band is
;                         still not offered and because a range that
;                         silently disappears from a map gets re-used by
;                         somebody who never knew it was claimed.
;   $3B300000..$3FFFFFFF  not DRAM - the hole between the two banks
;   $40000000..$FBFFFFFF  #PAY1_LO..#PAY1_HI  HIGH window, 3 GiB
;   $FC000000..$FFFFFFFF  VideoCore and the peripheral aperture
;
; WHY THE LOW WINDOW STILL STOPS AT $07EFFFFF. Every image this compiler
; emits sets sp to $08000000 unless it was built with --stack-addr, and
; it grows DOWN from there. A payload loaded at $07F80000 would be eaten
; by its own first push. One clear megabyte below the default stack top
; is the same margin the proven monitor used and it is kept for the same
; reason.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE CEILING AND WHERE IT WENT
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; MEASURED 2026-08-28, twice, because the number in circulation before
; that date was wrong in a way that stopped work: it was believed that
; this monitor sat about three kilobytes from a wall and therefore could
; not host Wi-Fi. It does not, and it can. Everything below is a build
; rather than an opinion, and the build lines are at the end of this
; section so anybody can reproduce every number in it with no board.
;
; RE-MEASURE RATHER THAN TRUST A FIGURE WRITTEN HERE BEFORE 2026-08-28.
; The A64 backend gained PruneUnreachedProcedures (IRCore_A64.pbi) that
; morning and images are 8-37 percent smaller than any earlier figure.
; Where an old number is still quoted below it is labelled as historical
; and the current one is beside it.
;
; THERE WERE TWO NUMBERS AND ONLY ONE OF THEM WAS A CEILING. The soft one
; is still here and still soft. The hard one has been removed - see THE
; HARD ONE below, which is the long half of this section.
;
; THE SOFT ONE - the BSS floor at #MON_LO + 512 KiB. It is a NOTCH, not
; a wall. A64Assembler.pbi runs a measuring pass before it assembles
; (A64Assembler.pbi:1892-1919): pass 0 walks the whole listing to find
; where the code really ends, throws the symbol table away, and places
; the whole BSS on the NEXT 64 KiB BOUNDARY after it - never below
; A64LoadBase + #A64_BSS_GAP (A64Assembler.pbi:31, $00080000), which is
; a floor kept only so that a small program keeps the layout it has
; always had. Code passing $00280000 therefore collides with nothing;
; it moves BSS to $00290000 and the build carries on.
;
; THAT HAS ALREADY HAPPENED HERE. The image was 548,964 bytes on the
; morning of 2026-08-28, which ends at $00286064 - twenty-four kilobytes
; PAST the floor - and the .sym for that same build puts the first BSS
; symbol at $00290000. The mechanism did its job, said nothing, and the
; report that this file was three kilobytes from disaster was describing
; a build that had already been superseded.
;
; THE NOTCH NO LONGER APPLIES TO THIS BUILD AT ALL, and the paragraphs
; above are kept because they apply to every OTHER program on this
; target - the diagnostics, the harnesses, anything built without
; --bss-addr. This file's build line names a BSS base explicitly, so the
; measuring pass still runs (the overlap refusal needs its answer) but
; does not get to place anything. See A64SetBssBase in
; RaspberryPi4/A64Assembler.pbi.
;
; WHAT MOVING THE NOTCH COSTS, since a bare claim that nothing breaks
; deserves a list rather than a promise:
;
;   the stack refusal      still correct. A64Assembler.pbi:1978-1988
;                          tests the stack top against the MEASURED end
;                          of BSS, not against the floor, so it tracks
;                          the notch by construction.
;   the .sym contract      unchanged. BSS labels stay absolute and code
;                          labels stay image-relative either way
;                          (A64Assembler.pbi:1990-1999), so every gate
;                          under tools/a64 reads the right addresses
;                          without knowing where the notch landed.
;   the native emulator    A64EmuCore.pbi:73-74 still computes its BSS
;                          base as load + 512 KiB unconditionally, so
;                          for an image LARGER than 512 KiB it disagrees
;                          with the assembler. It does not misroute a
;                          real BSS access - the model's RAM segment is
;                          8 MiB and begins at the lower of the two, so
;                          the true BSS is inside it - but in the band
;                          where image and RAM overlap a read is served
;                          from the image and a write goes to the RAM
;                          array. Nothing in this monitor is exercised
;                          there: the oracle that runs this file is
;                          tools/a64/a64_interp.py, which has one flat
;                          memory and no such seam. Recorded here as a
;                          known divergence rather than repaired from a
;                          file that cannot test the repair.
;
; THE HARD ONE - #AB_BASE - WAS THE CEILING, AND IT IS GONE. TWICE.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; 2026-08-28 TOOK HALF OF IT OUT AND THE NOTE OF THAT DATE SAID IT HAD
; TAKEN ALL OF IT. Read the correction before anything else in this
; section: what moved that day was the BSS, which stopped sharing the
; window with the code (--bss-addr, #MON_DATA_LO). The CODE window
; stayed exactly as it was - #MON_LO $00200000 to #MON_HI $003FFFFF,
; with the autoboot record in its last 256 bytes, the payload window
; starting at the constant $00400000 and every upload tool staging
; there. So the budget for the program went from 2,096,896 bytes for
; code AND BSS to 2,096,896 bytes for code alone. That is a bigger
; allowance. It is not the absence of one, and it was reported as the
; absence of one.
;
; ELEVEN DAYS LATER THE MONITOR WAS 2,083,268 BYTES. Sixteen thousand
; eight hundred and seventy-two bytes of headroom - about one commit -
; and the overrun would have been an image landing on the autoboot
; record with nothing printed. The half that was left was the half that
; mattered.
;
; THE RULING, 2026-09-08: "the bootloader is a kernel and can be as big
; as it wants." That is not a bigger number. It is the removal of the
; idea that there is a number.
;
; WHAT A CEILING ACTUALLY WAS, so the shape is recognisable next time.
; It was never one constant. It was a MAP BUILT AROUND A CONSTANT: the
; program's reserved region was typed rather than measured, and then
; four other things - the autoboot record, the payload window, the
; staging address in four host tools, the gate's layout check - were
; each positioned against that typed number. Every one of them had to
; be right about a fact none of them could see, and the program was the
; only thing in the arrangement that knew how big it was.
;
; SO THE PROGRAM ANSWERS NOW. RaspberryPi4/EmitCore_A64.pbi emits
; __image_start__ and __image_end__ on every A64 build - the pair costs
; zero bytes, an image hashes identically before and after - and
; Board/memmap.pi4 reads them with the same adrp/add pair _start uses
; for __bss_start__. From that one measurement:
;
;   HwMonLo()/HwMonHi()   the running image, rounded up to a megabyte.
;                         Not #MON_LO: a monitor relocated with
;                         --load-addr now protects ITSELF, which the
;                         constants got backwards in both directions -
;                         see BUILDING THIS SOMEWHERE OTHER THAN
;                         $200000 below, where that defect was recorded
;                         as unfixable and no longer is.
;   HwPayLo(0)            the megabyte above #MON_LO plus the image
;                         size. The window is bounded below by the
;                         monitor that will be resident after the next
;                         reset, which is the one at the load address -
;                         so a relocated copy still advertises the
;                         window a payload was built for.
;   HwStageAddr()         that same address, and it is what the four
;                         upload tools now ASK the board for through
;                         `map` instead of each carrying 0x400000.
;   the region list       HwMonRegions()/HwMonRegionLo()/HwMonRegionHi()
;                         - the code, the data window and the autoboot
;                         page. HitsMonitor() walks it instead of
;                         growing an If per board.
;
; THE GRAIN IS ONE MEGABYTE AND THAT IS THE ONLY GUARD. A payload
; address stays valid until the monitor crosses a megabyte boundary,
; and on the day it does the board says so in a full sentence with the
; new window in it. The whole arithmetic is in Board/memmap.pi4 with
; its reasoning; nothing here restates it, because a second copy of a
; rule is the defect this removed.
;
; THE AUTOBOOT RECORD MOVED, and this paragraph supersedes THE THREE
; OBVIOUS MOVES above. That list refused "move the autoboot record out"
; on the grounds that it buys 256 bytes and that there is nowhere to
; put it. Both were answers to a different question. It is not buying
; space now - it is getting out of the way of a region with no top -
; and "nowhere to put it" was about a WATCHDOG SCRATCH REGISTER that
; survives a reset of the SoC, which this part genuinely does not have
; and which the record never needed. It needs raw DRAM at a fixed
; address that survives _start. $001FF000, the last page below the load
; address, is that: the image grows UP from #MON_LO so no size reaches
; it, the monitor's stack grows DOWN from $00100000 a megabyte below
; it, and it is not BSS so the zeroing loop cannot touch it. The four
; properties and the reasoning are in Board/memmap.pi4.
;
; THE OTHER TWO REFUSALS IN THAT LIST STAND AS WRITTEN. Growing the
; window upward and growing it downward were both answers to "how do we
; get a bigger allowance", and there is no allowance to enlarge.
;
; WHAT THIS COSTS, item by item, because a claim that nothing breaks
; deserves a list rather than a promise:
;
;   today's map               MOVED, and that is measured rather than
;                             predicted. When this lane began the image
;                             was 2,083,268 bytes and its arithmetic
;                             landed on $00200000..$003FFFFF with the
;                             window at $00400000 - the exact two
;                             constants this file used to carry, which
;                             is why the proof gate below had to exist.
;                             With main merged it is 2,106,560 bytes,
;                             ending at $004024BF, so it reserves
;                             $00200000..$004FFFFF and the window is at
;                             $00500000. The constants were sixteen
;                             kilobytes from wrong when the ruling was
;                             made and they went wrong eleven hours
;                             later, in somebody else's branch.
;   a payload built last month  STILL RUNS, and does not need
;                             rebuilding. The address in a build line
;                             does not go into the image: the same
;                             source built --load-addr 0x400000 and
;                             --load-addr 0x1000000 is byte for byte
;                             the same file, because A64 output is
;                             position-independent - measured on six
;                             diagnostics and gated whole-image by
;                             a64_nosizecap_check case 9. What moves is
;                             where it is STAGED, which the board says
;                             and the tools ask. An address typed by
;                             hand at the old window is refused loudly,
;                             with the new one printed.
;   the four upload tools     ask `map` and follow. tools/anvil.py's
;                             stage_from_map is the single reader of
;                             that answer; a monitor too old to have
;                             `map` gets a full sentence and the run
;                             stops rather than guessing.
;   the gates                 tools/a64/a64_anvil_check.py computes the
;                             map from #MON_LO, #MON_GRAIN and the
;                             length of the built image, then runs the
;                             monitor's own `map` and grades every
;                             address against it - two statements of
;                             one rule, one of them executed.
;   the proof                 tools/a64/a64_nosizecap_check.py builds
;                             this monitor with a megabyte of real
;                             table added, gets an image half again the
;                             size of the old ceiling, and shows the
;                             map, the window, the staging address and
;                             a run all moving with it while the
;                             autoboot record stays where it is. That
;                             gate exists because every number in the
;                             other one is, today, also consistent with
;                             a ceiling that never left.
;   the command-line build    MaxROM is 0 for pi4 and unoq. It was a
;                             64 MiB "soft image-size budget" on parts
;                             with no ROM.
;   the native emulator       no longer caps an image at 8 MiB, and its
;                             BSS base follows --bss-addr. That
;                             divergence was recorded as survivable
;                             while images were small; at two megabytes
;                             load + 512 KiB is inside the image and
;                             the model would answer a read and a write
;                             of one address from two places.
;
; WHAT IS STILL WORTH DOING, AND IT IS NOT URGENT ANY MORE
; --------------------------------------------------------
; HOW MANY SCRATCHPAD PAGES THE VL805 ACTUALLY ASKS FOR HAS NEVER BEEN
; WRITTEN DOWN. It is a field of HCSPARAMS2 and xhci.pi4:1530 already
; reads it into xh_scratch, so the board can answer the question the
; moment somebody asks it. If the VL805 wants 32, #XHCI_MAX_SCRATCH = 32
; hands 384 KiB back. That used to be the single largest saving
; available anywhere in this program; it is now just tidiness, which is
; the point of having done the layout work first.
;
; IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED ON A GUESS. Handing a controller fewer
; scratchpad buffers than it asks for is a DMA master writing into
; memory that is not its own, and xhci.pi4:1984-1985 refuses loudly for
; exactly that reason. Lowering the constant from a plausible number
; would trade that loud refusal for silent corruption, which is the
; wrong direction on a bench where the corruption would be blamed on
; the USB stack for a week. Measure it, then change it.
;
; DOES WI-FI FIT. YES, and the question is now the wrong shape - the
; whole-tree build above includes sdio.pi4 and cyw43.pi4 along with
; twenty-eight others and lands at 59 percent of the OLD budget, which
; is the budget for CODE ALONE now. The older answer here quoted 680,768
; bytes for anvil-plus-those-two and called it a worst case "because
; this backend does not yet drop procedures the program cannot reach".
; It does now, so that sentence and its number are both retired; see the
; whole-tree measurement above, which supersedes them.
;
; THE FIRMWARE BLOB IS NOT PART OF THIS ARITHMETIC. brcmfmac43455-sdio.bin
; is 609,309 bytes and it stays a FILE on the boot stick that fat.pi4
; reads at run time; it is never linked and never appears in the image.
; It does need somewhere to land, and that somewhere must NOT be this
; window - it is nearly a third of it. The payload low window has
; 123 MiB (#PAY0_LO, $00400000) and is the obvious home. Whoever wires
; the upload should name a constant for it down beside #ETH_RX_BASE
; rather than taking the obvious next free address: two multicore
; examples once did exactly that and silently overwrote a live string
; that belonged to something else.
;
; IS THE IMAGE SIZE ITSELF A CONSTRAINT, separately from the layout? No,
; and it is worth saying because the two get confused. 549 KB over `b`
; at the measured 37 KB/s is about fifteen seconds, and 680 KB is about
; nineteen. That is an annoyance, and the answer to it already exists
; and is not a diet: `get` fetches over the Ethernet socket instead,
; which is the whole reason the network went in - eleven kilobytes a
; second was twenty-four seconds for a 260 KB image. The boot path does
; not care either. The firmware loads KERNEL8.IMG whole, off a USB
; stick rather than a wire.
;
; THE BUILD LINES, so every number above can be reproduced with no board:
;
;   the monitor as it ships - the placement comes from this file's own
;   LoadAddress / BssAddress / StackAddress lines, so there is nothing
;   to pass
;     PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4/Board/board.pi4 -t pi4
;         -o RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.img
;
;   the same thing with BSS BACK INSIDE THE IMAGE, which is what the
;   54-percent figure above describes and is the layout every earlier
;   number in this file was measured in. It can no longer be produced
;   by leaving a flag off, because the source now says where BSS goes:
;   comment out the BssAddress line and build. (A --bss-addr flag will
;   not do it either - one that disagrees with the source is refused.)
;     PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4/Board/board.pi4 -t pi4
;         -o anvil_contiguous.img
;
;   the whole-tree build, which is the 59-percent figure: add an
;   XIncludeFile line for every .pi4 in RaspberryPi4/Lib except soc.pi4,
;   then build a copy the same way. Vulkan viewport defaults are encoded as
;   explicit IEEE-zero bit patterns and do not enable floating point.
;
; then read the size of the .img for the code, and the .sym with the
; .sym.meta for the BSS: every symbol at or above the load address is a
; BSS symbol at an ABSOLUTE address, and the .meta line of the same name
; gives its size in bytes. The end of BSS is the largest address plus
; size among those. Nothing here needs the board to be powered.
;
; THAT "AT OR ABOVE THE LOAD ADDRESS" RULE STILL HOLDS WITH --bss-addr,
; and it is worth saying because it is the one thing about the .sym that
; could have broken. Code labels are exported as offsets from the load
; base and BSS labels as absolute addresses
; (A64Assembler.pbi's ExportSymFile call); a BSS base ABOVE the load
; address keeps the two sets far apart, which $0D000000 does by 206 MiB.
; A BSS base BELOW the load address would make the rule ambiguous, and
; that is a reason to keep this one above it rather than a defect.
;
; WHY $08100000..$3B2FFFFF IS LEFT OUT even though it is free. Both of
; its edges MOVE. The bottom edge is wherever the payload's stack top
; ends up, which --stack-addr can change at build time and the monitor
; cannot discover. The top edge is U-Boot's relocation base, which moves
; when U-Boot is updated or when the board's DRAM size changes - and it
; is not just dead code up there: U-Boot leaves the PSCI / spin-table
; trampoline that cores 1-3 are parked in, and those cores are still
; executing it. A window whose edges move is a window that is wrong one
; day without announcing it. The high window has no such edges.
;
; WHY THE HIGH WINDOW IS THE WHOLE OF BANK 1. Nothing of U-Boot's is in
; bank 1 at all: it relocated into bank 0 and reserves upward within
; bank 0, so there is no reservation in bank 1 to collide with, and no
; number in this file that has to track U-Boot's size. 3 GiB, one
; constant each end, both measured.
;
; A BLOCK MAY NOT STRADDLE THE GAP between the windows, and the range
; check below refuses one that tries rather than writing the half that
; happens to land. There is no DRAM at $3B300000..$3FFFFFFF; a store
; there is not slow, it is wrong.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; BUILDING THIS SOMEWHERE OTHER THAN $200000 - READ BEFORE YOU DO
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; A RESIDENT MONITOR REFUSES A LOAD INTO $200000..$3FFFFF, which is its
; own window, so it cannot be used to replace itself. That refusal is
; correct and it is the whole point of HitsMonitor(). The two ways round
; it, and they are not equal:
;
;   through U-Boot   reset, let U-Boot come up, load and "go 0x200000".
;                    This is the canonical build and the one every
;                    number in the map above describes. It costs a
;                    power cycle or a reset.
;
;   somewhere else   a build with --load-addr 0x2000000 puts a second copy
;                    in the LOW PAYLOAD WINDOW, where the resident
;                    monitor is willing to write it, and "g 2000000"
;                    hands over with no reset at all.
;
; THE PROTECTED REGION FOLLOWS THE RUNNING IMAGE SINCE 2026-09-08, and
; this paragraph used to say the opposite. It said: "#MON_LO/#MON_HI are
; constants and do not follow the image. A copy running at $2000000
; still refuses loads at $200000..$3FFFFF - the window where nothing of
; it lives - and does NOT refuse a load on top of itself" - and closed
; with "making the window follow the image would need the running base
; at run time, and this file does not know it".
;
; IT KNOWS IT NOW. __image_start__ and __image_end__ are emitted into
; every A64 image and HwMonLo()/HwMonHi() read them, so a copy running
; at $2000000 protects $2000000 and nothing else. Both halves of the old
; defect are gone at once: it no longer guards a window it is not in,
; and it no longer allows a payload on top of itself. The banner names
; the running image for the same reason.
;
; THE PAYLOAD WINDOW IS DELIBERATELY NOT MOVED BY A RELOCATED COPY. It
; is computed from #MON_LO plus the image's SIZE, not from where this
; copy happens to be, because the window answers "where may a payload
; live" and that band is bounded below by the monitor that will be
; resident after the next reset. So a payload aimed at the window this
; board advertises still works while a second monitor is running at
; $2000000, and a load aimed at $2000000 is refused by name. The
; reasoning is at HwPayLo() in Board/memmap.pi4. Type `map` for the
; address rather than reading one off this page: on the build this
; paragraph was last measured against it is $00500000, and the point of
; the whole change is that the next build may say something else.
;
; A relocated copy is still a fine way to try a new monitor on a live
; bench and still a bad thing to leave running: put the canonical build
; back at $200000 at the next reset, and type `map` on whichever one is
; answering to see which computer you are actually talking to.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; RUNNING A PAYLOAD IN THE HIGH WINDOW - what it needs from the build
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; NOTHING, unless it pins its BSS. The load address is not in the image:
; code reaches its own code, data and variables PC-relative, so the same
; source built --load-addr 0x400000 and --load-addr 0x40000000 is byte
; for byte the same file, and a payload built for the low window runs
; unchanged when it is staged in the high one (proved whole-image by
; tools/a64/a64_nosizecap_check.py case 9, and entered at a staging
; address it was not built for by case 8). An image built with an
; explicit --bss-addr or BssAddress line IS pinned: its variables are at
; that absolute address and the code finds them by distance, so that one
; has to be built for the window it will run in:
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile ... -t pi4 --load-addr 0x40000000
;
; ONE THING THE WINDOW CHECK DOES NOT COVER, and it is easy to miss: the
; image's BSS is placed 512 KiB above its load base (#A64_BSS_GAP), and
; the loader never writes a byte of it, so no range check here ever sees
; it. A payload staged near the TOP of a window puts its BSS past the
; end of that window at run time, and in the low window's case that is
; the payload's own stack corridor. Leave room, or use the high window,
; where there are three gigabytes of it.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE STACK COLLISION, AND WHY THIS FILE MOVES ITS OWN STACK
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; Every image the compiler emits starts with a prologue that sets sp to
; a HARDCODED $08000000 - monitor and payload alike. Left alone, the
; payload's very first push lands on the monitor's live frames and
; returning from "g" walks off into the weeds.
;
; The first thing Main() does is therefore move the monitor's stack to
; #MON_STACK with three instructions of inline asm. From that point on
; every monitor frame lives near $00100000 and the payload is welcome
; to own $08000000 undisturbed. This is why Main() MUST NEVER RETURN:
; its own frame was allocated on the old stack and its epilogue would
; reload a link register from the new one. Main() ends in an endless
; command loop with no Break for exactly that reason.
;
; THE LINK-TIME STACK TOP IS NOW STATED IN THIS FILE, as StackAddress
; $8000000 under WHERE THIS IMAGE RUNS below. That is the compiler
; default and the value this file has always been built with, so nothing
; moved; it is written out so the file states its whole layout in one
; place instead of two thirds of it.
;
; IT IS NOT THE STACK THE MONITOR RUNS ON. The asm block below overrides
; it a few instructions into Main(), which is the entire point of that
; block - so StackAddress here is what a PAYLOAD inherits, not what the
; monitor uses. Changing it (or passing --stack-addr, which must agree
; with it or the build stops) does not move the monitor's stack; delete
; the asm block if that is what you want.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; LANGUAGE FACTS THIS FILE IS WRITTEN AROUND (64-bit model)
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; * .i, pointers and every integer intermediate are 8 bytes, SIGNED,
;   carried in X registers. Every physical address a BCM2711 can produce
;   fits in 40 bits, so it is positive in a 64-bit .i and the signed
;   comparisons in this file are correct for every address the hardware
;   can present. That is a statement about the part, not a lucky
;   accident of the type.
; * A PEEK FOLLOWS ITS TYPE LETTER, ruled 2026-08-25, and this file was
;   swept for it. PeekB/PeekW/PeekL name the SIGNED types and they
;   SIGN-EXTEND; PeekA/PeekU/PeekN are the unsigned spellings. So:
;     - a raw payload byte is PeekA, not "PeekB(...) & $FF". The mask
;       gave the right answer and said the wrong thing.
;     - a peripheral register is PeekN. The VideoCore's success code IS
;       $80000000 and its FULL flag IS bit 31; read through PeekL those
;       are negative numbers.
;     - reading a .b ARRAY element (gLine[], gRec[]) is a .b read and
;       still sign-extends, so those masks stay. The accessor changed;
;       the type did not.
; * A BARE Peek/Poke, and .i, ARE EIGHT BYTES on this target. PeekI /
;   PokeI are the explicit spellings and the autoboot record uses them.
; * >> IS ARITHMETIC on a signed .i. Every value shifted right in this
;   file is provably positive at the point of the shift (a masked hex
;   nibble, a revision bitfield, or a CRC accumulator held inside 32
;   bits), and the sites that depend on it say so.
; * Procedure locals are statically allocated, not stacked. Nothing here
;   is reentrant and nothing recurses - both loaders, the command parser
;   and the boot wait are iterative loops.
; * OPERATOR PRECEDENCE IS NOT the desktop BASIC's. & | ! bind TIGHTER than
;   << >>, so "v << 4 | d" would parse as "v << (4|d)" and
;   "crc >> 1 ! POLY" would parse as "crc >> (1 ! POLY)". Written
;   "(v << 4) | d" and "(crc >> 1) ! POLY" throughout. Parenthesise
;   shifts, always.
; * A CONSTANT INITIALISER TAKES A BARE LITERAL. "#K = 2 + 1" is
;   refused, which is why the sizes below are written out.
; * .q is accepted by this backend. THIS FILE DOES NOT USE IT. .i is
;   already the 64-bit machine word here.
;
; =====================================================================
;  THE TWO DOWNLOAD PATHS
; =====================================================================
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; b - THE FAST PATH. Our own protocol, because it can be.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;   pmf> b <addr-hex> <len-hex> <crc32-hex>
;   monitor:  rdy
;   host:     <len> raw bytes, in chunks of 1024
;   monitor:  one "+" after each chunk, including the last short one
;   monitor:  ok        (verified)     or     crc     (did not verify)
;
; MEASURED 11.2 KB/s against S-records' 4.6. Nothing in the 2.0 rewrite
; touches the protocol, and #BLK MUST KEEP MATCHING RAW_CHUNK in
; tools/ubsend.py - they are one number written in two places because
; the two programs cannot share a header.
;
; ASCII HEADER, BINARY BODY. The header stays human-readable so the
; protocol is greppable in a terminal log and can be driven by hand from
; a serial console. The body is raw, which is the entire point: an
; S-record spends two hex characters plus per-line framing on every
; byte.
;
; THE ADDRESS FIELD IS AS WIDE AS THE PARSER, which is 16 hex digits.
; Every byte of DRAM on a 4 GiB Pi 4 is below $100000000, so an S3
; record can already reach all of it; the width here is for an 8 GiB
; part and for anything mapped high.
;
; PER-CHUNK ACK, AND NOT A BYTE MORE OF PROTOCOL. There is no flow
; control on this wire and the PL011 has a 32-byte RX FIFO - about 2.7
; ms of slack at 115200. One ack byte per kilobyte costs about a
; millisecond against the ~89 ms that kilobyte takes to arrive, so the
; insurance is free. It is still NOT a state machine with retries,
; sequence numbers and per-chunk timeouts. A host that ignores the acks
; entirely still transfers correctly; it just loses the backstop.
;
; CRC32 OVER THE WHOLE IMAGE, NOT PER CHUNK. A per-record checksum -
; which is all an S-record has - cannot detect a MISSING record. A whole
; image CRC catches truncation, reordering and corruption with one
; number. Standard IEEE polynomial, reflected, $EDB88320, so the host
; side is `zlib.crc32(data)` and nothing else.
;
; IT IS COMPUTED BY READING THE PAYLOAD BACK OUT OF MEMORY after the
; transfer, not accumulated as bytes arrive. Two reasons and both are
; real: it keeps the receive loop down to "poll, read, store", which is
; the loop that has a deadline; and it verifies what the stores actually
; produced rather than what we believed we received.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; l - THE PROVEN PATH. Kept, deliberately.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; S3 data records and an S7 terminator. It is the path that got the
; first image onto this board and the path that carried the
; --entry-returns proof on 2026-08-25, and it is not being deleted. If
; `b` turns out to have a timing problem on the real wire that no model
; can show, the board still has a loader. If the two ever disagree about
; the same image, that disagreement is information - dump the range with
; `m` and believe the CRC.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE HOST SIDE
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; tools/ubsend.py drives both. Its prompt tuple already includes ours:
;
;   PROMPTS = (b"U-Boot> ", b"=> ", b"pmf> ")
;
; DO NOT CHANGE THE PROMPT STRING. The host waits for it. The S-record
; mode needs nothing else, because it sends "loads", which this monitor
; reads as the l command (the word after the first letter is ignored),
; and "go 0x200000", which it reads as g with a 0x-prefixed argument.
; That U-Boot compatibility is deliberate and it is cheap, so it stays -
; and it is why the new commands are all single letters that no U-Boot
; script would type by accident.
;
; =====================================================================
;  WHAT 2.0 ADDS, AND WHY EACH ONE IS HERE
; =====================================================================
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; i - board info
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; Revision decoded, SoC temperature, and every clock's configured AND
; measured rate. All of it is mailbox.pi4 talking to the VideoCore; none
; of it is new work here.
;
; THE REVISION TABLE STOPS AT THE BCM2711 FAMILY on purpose. A decoder
; that guesses a model it has never met is a confident wrong answer, and
; the processor field is printed separately anyway, so a board this
; table does not know still reports its SoC and its memory correctly and
; says "board type $xx" for the part it cannot name. Extend the table
; when a board is actually in front of you, not from a web page.
;
; TEMPERATURE IS FAHRENHEIT (house rule 20). The mailbox reports
; THOUSANDTHS OF A DEGREE CELSIUS and mailbox.pi4 deliberately does not
; convert - that is the documented unit and hiding it would make the
; library unreadable against the firmware documentation. The conversion
; happens once, for the whole tree, in Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi's
; HwTempMilliF(); PutTempF() only formats it. Hz is left alone: hertz is
; the American engineer's unit as much as anyone's, which is exactly the
; exception rule 20 states.
;
; CONFIGURED AND MEASURED ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS. The configured rate
; is what the firmware intends; the measured rate is what it observes.
; They disagree whenever the on-demand governor has dropped the core for
; thermal or undervoltage reasons. Printing only one of them would be
; the kind of half-truth this project exists to stop.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; c [hz] - show or set the ARM clock
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; This board BOOTS AT 600 MHz while the compiler's board file says
; 1500000000 - measured 2026-08-25, a factor of 2.5 with nothing
; anywhere saying so. `c` is how you find out and `c 1500000000` is how
; you fix it, through clocks.pi4.
;
; THE FIRMWARE CLAMPS A RATE IT DISLIKES AND REPORTS SUCCESS. Ask for
; 2.5 GHz on a 1.5 GHz part and the response code is SUCCESS and the
; rate you get is 1500000000. So this command prints what was ASKED,
; what was APPLIED and what was then MEASURED, and never just "ok". If
; applied differs from asked it says CLAMPED in as many words.
;
; ITS ARGUMENT IS DECIMAL, and it is the only decimal argument in this
; monitor. Every address here is hex because addresses are read in hex;
; a clock rate is read in decimal by every human and every datasheet,
; and $59682F00 is not a number anyone types on purpose. A leading $ or
; 0x still switches to hex if you want it. The help text says so.
;
; RAISING THE CLOCK DOES NOT SHORTEN ANY TIMEOUT IN THIS FILE. Every one
; of them is counted in CNTPCT_EL0 ticks through timer.pi4, and that
; counter is fixed at 54 MHz and independent of the CPU - MEASURED, not
; assumed: the same delay at 600 MHz and at 1.5 GHz came back 10800007
; against 10800003 ticks. That independence is the reason the receive
; timeout is a tick count and not a spin count. uart.pi4's
; UartReadWait() takes a spin count, which is the right shape for a
; library that must not depend on a clock; it is the WRONG shape here,
; because `c` can change the CPU speed underneath it by 2.5x and the
; transfer timeout would silently change with it.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; w <addr> <value> - write one 32-bit word
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; The companion to `m`, and the thing you reach for when a peripheral
; needs one bit poked to prove a theory. It refuses an address inside
; the monitor, exactly as both loaders do, and it refuses an unaligned
; one, because a 32-bit store to an odd address on a device aperture is
; an alignment fault rather than a slow write.
;
; IT IS NOT WINDOW-CHECKED, for the same reason `m` is not: being able
; to point it at a register is most of its value. Reading and writing an
; address with no device behind it can hang the bus, and that risk is
; accepted here as it is there.
;
; IT PRINTS THE READ-BACK, and the read-back is NOT a verdict. Half the
; registers worth writing are write-only, write-one-to-clear, or latch
; something other than what you wrote. What the line tells you is what
; the device returns when asked, which is a fact; whether that equals
; what you wrote is your call, not the monitor's.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; a <addr> and the boot delay - AUTOBOOT
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; This is the U-Boot habit people would otherwise keep U-Boot for: on
; start, wait a couple of seconds, and if nobody objects, run the thing
; that is meant to run.
;
; THE DEFAULT IS SAFE AND IT IS THE ONLY DEFAULT THAT CAN BE. Getting
; the "no key pressed" path wrong strands a board that somebody has to
; physically walk to. So:
;
;   nothing armed  ->  NO WAIT AT ALL, straight to the prompt. A
;                      countdown that can only ever expire into "do
;                      nothing" is two seconds of annoyance per boot and
;                      teaches people to ignore it. U-Boot with an empty
;                      bootcmd does the same.
;   armed          ->  print the address, count down about two seconds
;                      with a dot every quarter second, and stop dead on
;                      any keypress. The key is CONSUMED, so it does not
;                      become the first character of a command.
;   armed at an    ->  refused at the prompt when you arm it, and
;   address inside     refused again at boot time. The record lives in
;   the monitor        DRAM and DRAM can be stale; a check that only
;                      runs on the way in is a check that a power cycle
;                      can walk around.
;
; THE RECORD CANNOT BE A Global, and this is the trap the whole project
; keeps re-learning. A Global lives in BSS and _start ZEROES BSS four
; instructions in, so an arming written before a restart would be erased
; by the code meant to preserve it. Lib/safety.pi4 hit the identical
; trap from the other side the same week and keeps its boot counter in
; raw DRAM for the same reason. Generalised in the vault as: on this
; backend, anything that must survive _start cannot be a Global.
;
; THAT PARAGRAPH IS NARROWER THAN IT READS, CORRECTED 2026-08-28. An
; ORDINARY Global is zeroed by _start. A Global declared NoClear is not:
; the emitter lays those out AFTER __bss_end__ and the zeroing loop stops
; there (EmitCore_A64.pbi:861-910, and the addresses rather than the
; section names are what tools/a64/a64_noclear_check.py proves). So "on
; this backend, anything that must survive _start cannot be a Global" is
; no longer true as stated, and somebody re-deriving it from this file
; would build a worse thing than the language offers.
;
; THE RECORD STILL IS NOT ONE, and the reason is a different one than
; the reason it was written for. A NoClear Global's ADDRESS is wherever
; the compiler puts it, which moves whenever anything above it in the
; layout changes size. That is fine for safety.pi4's boot counter, which
; only ever has to be read by the same build that wrote it. It is wrong
; for a record whose entire job is to be found after a reset by an image
; that may have been rebuilt in between: a moved record is not a
; corrupted record, it is an ABSENT one, and absent reads as "nothing
; armed", which is the safe answer but also the silently wrong one.
; #AB_BASE is a number that does not move.
;
; WHERE IT IS, AND WHY IT LEFT THE IMAGE WINDOW ON 2026-09-08. It was
; $003FFF00, the last 256 bytes of a two-megabyte window - which made it
; THE CEILING, because it was the first thing a growing image would land
; on and the landing was silent. Once the window stopped having a top
; there was no such place to keep it, so it went BELOW the load address
; instead: $001FF000, the last page under #MON_LO. An image grows UP from
; its base, so there is no number of bytes of code that arrives at an
; address beneath it; the monitor's own stack grows DOWN from $00100000,
; a megabyte the other way; and it is not BSS, so _start cannot reach it
; either. Board/memmap.pi4 states all four properties beside the
; constant.
;
; SO IT IS THREE WORDS OF RAW DRAM at #AB_BASE. It is no longer inside
; the image window, so the loader refusal no longer protects it for
; free - it is DECLARED instead, as one of the regions HwMonRegion*()
; names and HitsMonitor() refuses, which is what stops `w` and `fill`
; from quietly disarming an armed board:
;
;   +0   #AB_MAGIC          "PAYLOAD!" as a 64-bit literal
;   +8   the entry address
;   +16  entry ! #AB_MAGIC  the guard
;
; TWO WORDS WOULD NOT DO. Uninitialised DRAM is not random - it is
; whatever was there before - and a magic alone can be met by a stale
; copy of a record whose address field has since been overwritten. The
; guard makes the pair have to agree, so a half-surviving record is
; rejected instead of jumped to.
;
; AND IT IS CLEANED TO THE POINT OF COHERENCY, "dc civac", not the "dc
; cvau" the payload flush uses. cvau cleans to the point of UNIFICATION,
; which is what instruction fetch needs and is not what surviving a
; reset needs. Two instructions in the same family, one letter apart,
; and only one of them puts the bytes in DRAM.
;
; WHAT THIS CAN AND CANNOT BE TESTED WITHOUT A RESET, stated plainly:
; the wait, the keypress, the refusals and the record's own read/write
; are all exercisable from one prompt. Survival across a real reset is
; not, and is the one part of item 4 that only silicon settles.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; get, put and net - THE NETWORK
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; The last thing anybody was still opening U-Boot for. Eleven kilobytes
; a second down the serial line is twenty-four seconds for a 260 KB
; image, every single time somebody changes one line; the same image
; over this board's gigabit socket is well under a second.
;
;   get <name> [address]           fetch a file into memory
;   put <name> <address> <length>  send memory back out as a file
;   net ...                        the four addresses, and the wire
;
; It is TFTP, RFC 1350, which is what every board has been fetched over
; since 1981 and what U-Boot itself uses, so anything that can serve a
; file to a router can serve one to this.
;
; THE WHOLE OF THE REASONING IS IN THIS FILE, further down, under THE
; NETWORK - get, put and net. It is there rather than here because it is
; long and because it is about three libraries that live outside this
; file. What belongs in this header is the four things that change the
; shape of the monitor:
;
;   * THREE MORE LIBRARIES ARE INCLUDED and the image grew from about
;     380 KB to about 520 KB because of it. That is 46 seconds to send
;     over `b` rather than 34. It passed #MON_LO + 512 KiB shortly
;     afterwards and NOTHING HAPPENED, which is the designed behaviour
;     and not a near miss. THE CEILING AND WHERE IT WENT, in the memory
;     map section above, has the arithmetic - and has it because the
;     sentence that used to be here, that the image still fitted under
;     the BSS floor with a few kilobytes to spare, was read by a later
;     reader as a wall three kilobytes away and stopped work on the
;     strength of it.
;   * $08100000..$0817FFFF IS NOW SPOKEN FOR - the Ethernet receive
;     ring. It is inside the band the memory map above already refuses
;     to offer to a payload, so no window moved.
;   * `run` NOW STOPS THE ETHERNET before it jumps. A payload must not
;     share memory with a controller that is still writing into it.
;   * THE ADDRESSES LIVE IN THE SETTINGS STORE, under net.address,
;     net.netmask, net.gateway and net.server, exactly as the Wi-Fi keys
;     do - so `settings save` keeps them and nothing loads them for you.
;
; NONE OF IT HAS MOVED A FRAME. The Ethernet cable was unplugged from
; this board after transmit and receive were proven on 2026-08-26, and
; every claim about the network in this file is a reading of genet.pi4,
; net.pi4, tftp.pi4 and the nine RFCs in RaspberryPi4/Reference/. The
; one part a dark port CAN demonstrate is the refusal, and it is written
; to be demonstrated: five seconds of link wait, announced before it is
; taken, then a whole sentence saying the cable is out.
;
; =====================================================================
;  WHAT HAS ACTUALLY BEEN TESTED
; =====================================================================
; ON SILICON, 2026-08-25 morning, against version 1.x plus the
; --entry-returns backend change:
;
;   PASSED  g 0x400000 into a compiled payload built --entry-returns,
;           which RETURNED, and the prompt came back:
;             ## returned, x0 = 0337F980
;           $5A5A survived a 64-bit return, 0002 is CurrentEL>>2 (EL2),
;           $0337F980 is 54,000,000 from CNTFRQ_EL0.
;   PASSED  m 400000 64 afterwards, showing the epilogue in the Pi's own
;           RAM: FE 07 40 F9 F2 03 40 F9 5F 02 00 91 C0 03 5F D6
;   PASSED  the l S-record path carrying that payload in.
;   NOTE    the monitor resident that morning was the PRE-REWRITE build.
;           It answered "? unknown" to b and printed only EIGHT digits
;           of x0. Neither limitation is in this file.
;
; AGAINST THE PROJECT'S OWN A64 ORACLE, version 2.0, 2026-08-25. The
; image is EXECUTED - tools/a64/a64_interp.py with a PL011 and a
; VideoCore property mailbox modelled on top of it, and MRS added
; because the oracle does not decode it and timer.pi4 needs it. The
; mailbox model CLAMPS a clock request the way the firmware does, or
; the c command's three-number output would prove nothing.
;
;   122 assertions, all passing. What they cover:
;     banner, prompt, help, unknown command, blank line, Ctrl-C,
;     destructive backspace, and CRLF drawing one prompt not two
;     m against the image's own bytes and against the high window
;     w: the write, the read-back, the monitor refusal, the refusal of
;        a word that ENDS inside the monitor, and the alignment refusal
;     i: $00C03112 -> "Pi 4 Model B, BCM2711, 4 GB, rev 1.2", 48.732 C
;        printed as 119.7 F, and all four clocks configured + measured
;     c: bare report, a set that sticks, and a set that the firmware
;        CLAMPS from 2.5 GHz to 1.5 GHz while reporting success
;     a: arm, clear, report, the monitor refusal, a stale record aimed
;        at the monitor being refused AT BOOT, and a record whose guard
;        disagrees with its address being rejected
;     the boot delay: eight dots, then the payload runs and its x0 is
;        printed in sixteen digits; a keypress stops it and is consumed
;     g: the sixteen digits, "go 0x400000" (U-Boot's spelling), the
;        monitor refusal, and a 17-digit address refused not truncated
;     b: rdy, the ack per chunk, ok, crc, and every refusal - monitor,
;        both window edges, the gap between the banks, a 64-bit wrap,
;        a missing CRC - plus a wire that dies mid-transfer
;     l: records, bytes, range, entry, ok; a bad checksum; a record
;        aimed inside the monitor leaving the code intact; the HIGH
;        window; "loads"; and a silent host timing out
;
;   AND ONE FULL-SIZE TRANSFER, because a 512-byte test is not the same
;   claim: this monitor's own 45784-byte image was sent over b, 45 acks
;   came back, the CRC verified, and every one of the 45784 bytes was
;   compared in memory afterwards.
;
; THE MODEL FOUND ONE REAL DEFECT, in the first build of this file, and
; it is the kind that survives a code review: PutClockRow() took a
; "*name" parameter and did Print(*name), and the i command printed
; "2139496  configured 600000000" - the ADDRESS of the string, in
; decimal, with no diagnostic. Print()'s router picks its formatter from
; the argument's TYPE and an untyped pointer parameter is not a .s. The
; fix and the warning are at PutClockRow().
;
; STILL UNTESTED, and only silicon can settle them:
;   * the b protocol end to end, at 115200, against a real PL011 FIFO.
;     This is the one the model cannot answer: the whole reason for the
;     per-chunk ack is a timing risk a flat memory model does not have.
;   * the cache maintenance actually being needed, or sufficient. The
;     oracle has one flat memory and models dc/ic as no-ops, so the
;     maintenance words are DECODED but their EFFECT is not. That covers
;     the payload flush AND the autoboot record's civac.
;   * writes into the HIGH window at all. bdinfo says bank 1 is there;
;     that is a reading of a table, not a store that came back.
;   * the autoboot record surviving a real reset.
;   * whether U-Boot leaves anything at $00100000 that the monitor's
;     stack then lands on.
;   * EVERY PART OF get, put AND net THAT TOUCHES THE WIRE. The cable is
;     out. tools/a64/a64_anvil_check.py executes the two PURE decisions
;     in that work - the dotted-quad parser and the payload-window
;     lookup, 41 cases - by calling them directly in the interpreter
;     with no device model at all, because neither has any I/O. The
;     pump, the ARP resolve, the link refusals and the settings keys are
;     covered by nothing, and would need a modelled MAC, PHY, mailbox
;     and TFTP server at once. The three libraries underneath have their
;     own gates and all three pass.
;   * the mailbox read of this board's own MAC address (tag $00010003).
;     It has never been compared against the address printed on a real
;     board, and the fallback that runs when it fails has never run
;     either.

; ======================================================================
;  WHERE THIS IMAGE RUNS. THE FILE SAYS IT.                  2026-09-02
; ======================================================================
;  These three lines are the placement this program has always needed
;  and could previously only be GIVEN, on the compiler's command line:
;
;    --load-addr 0x200000 --bss-addr 0x0D000000
;
;  That was fine for a build script and impossible for the IDE, which
;  embeds the compiler and has no command line to put a flag on. An
;  Anvil built from the IDE compiled perfectly and was linked for
;  $80000 with its variables at $00280000 - inside the two-megabyte
;  window this image's code has to live in - and nothing said so. Now
;  the source states it, so the IDE build and the script build are the
;  same build.
;
;  The image and stack addresses stay fixed; BSS has moved to $0D000000
;  so the expanded monitor globals no longer exceed their protected range.
;  Each placement is argued for further down:
;
;    LoadAddress  - the firmware loads this whole image off the USB
;                   stick to $200000 as kernel8.img; CONFIG.TXT on the
;                   stick names the same address, and Lib/mmu.pi4:84
;                   says so. #MON_LO in Board/memmap.pi4 is this number
;                   written where the running program can see it.
;    BssAddress   - NOT OPTIONAL AND NOT A TUNING KNOB. See the section
;                   below: it is what puts this program's variables
;                   outside the window its code occupies, and
;                   #MON_DATA_LO is the same number again for the
;                   running program. Build without it and BSS returns
;                   to $00280000, inside the image.
;    StackAddress - the link-time initial stack top, which is the
;                   compiler default this file has always been built
;                   with. Main() then moves the monitor's own stack to
;                   #MON_STACK with three instructions of inline asm,
;                   for the reasons under THE STACK above - so this
;                   number is what a payload gets, not what the monitor
;                   runs on. It is written out rather than left implicit
;                   so this file states its whole layout in one place.
;
;  A --load-addr / --bss-addr / --stack-addr flag may still be passed,
;  and still wins where it is the only thing said. If a flag and one of
;  these lines DISAGREE the build stops and names both values - it never
;  quietly picks one.
; ======================================================================
LoadAddress  $200000
BssAddress   $0D000000
StackAddress $8000000

; ======================================================================
;  THE BUILD NUMBER (forum 586: `version` could not tell one build from
;  another)
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  #ANVIL_BUILD is an ordinary integer constant - CmdVersion() prints it
;  and it costs no RAM. The trailing marker is what makes the tools treat
;  it as THIS project's build number: a successful build in the IDE, or a
;  a `--bump-build` on the command line, raises it by exactly one and
;  writes that one line
;  back, byte for byte identical everywhere else.
;
;  IT IS DECLARED HERE, IN THE BOARD FILE, and not in Anvil/Core. The core
;  is shared by every board and a library that carried a build number
;  would be bumped by every project that includes it, which is exactly
;  what PMF-BLD-003 refuses. Each board file is its own project and counts
;  its own builds - the same shape as #ANVIL_V3D_CONSOLE below. A board
;  file that omits this line breaks the build at CmdVersion, loudly, which
;  is the right failure.
;
;  IT DOES NOT REPLACE THE CONTENT STAMP, and the two answer different
;  questions. The stamp (forum 621) says WHAT this image is - the digest
;  of the sources it was built from, so two builds of the same tree are
;  the same build. The number says WHICH build it is - it moves every time
;  somebody presses Compile, so a person watching a board can say "that is
;  not the one I just flashed" without hashing anything. `version` prints
;  both.
;
;  Bumping can be frozen for a release by writing `off` after the marker:
;      #ANVIL_BUILD = 1        ; pmf:build off
;  The IDE's Compiler > Build Number... window writes that word for you.
; ======================================================================
#ANVIL_BUILD = 210        ; pmf:build

;  The two optional siblings, written by the same successful build. They
;  are INTEGERS because `#NAME = <literal>` takes a number and nothing
;  else in this language - a string build stamp is not expressible as a
;  constant, and inventing one would be a language change nobody asked
;  for. YYYYMMDD and HHMMSS read correctly as decimal numbers anyway, and
;  CmdVersion() prints them with their separators put back.
#ANVIL_BUILD_DATE = 20260919   ; pmf:builddate
#ANVIL_BUILD_TIME = 031037     ; pmf:buildtime

; ======================================================================
;  THE LIBRARIES. A library never includes a library - the main file
;  lists what it needs, and the order matters where one calls another.
;  clocks.pi4 calls mailbox.pi4, so mailbox comes first.
; ======================================================================
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DRIVER-MODULE CONTRACT AND THE SEAM REGISTRY, AS EARLY AS THEY CAN
;  GO. All four are constants and one table; none of them reads hardware,
;  prints at boot, or calls anything else in this program.
;
;  THEY ARE HERE AND NOT BESIDE THE LOADER because a driver that a module
;  can REPLACE has to be able to ask, inside its own file, whether one
;  has - RaspberryPi4/Lib/thermal.pi4 does exactly that further down. The
;  alternative is a hook pointer bolted on beside each such driver and
;  set from below, which is a second path around a contract rather than
;  the contract.
;
;  seams.pbi is also the ONLY Anvil file a driver module includes: it is
;  the published contract, it carries no monitor source, and
;  RaspberryPi4/Modules/thermal_avs.pi4 is built against it alone.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/seams.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/module_format.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/module_runtime.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/mod_registry.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/wallclock.pbi"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/clocks.pi4"
; safety.pi4 is here for its PM/watchdog half only - the boot-count
; ladder is never called from this file. One overlap to know about:
; #SAFETY_COUNT_ADDR is $00001000, which is inside the window this
; monitor calls its stack ($1000..$FFFFF, growing DOWN from $100000).
; They cannot actually meet - the monitor would have to burn a full
; megabyte of stack to reach it - but the day someone calls
; SafetyBoot() from here, that is the collision to remember.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4"
; GPIO. The BCM2711 general-purpose I/O driver, wired in for the `gpio`
; command through the HwGpio* seam (RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_gpio.pi4 over
; this). It was in the tree, verified against the register tables, and no
; image had ever included it until now (its header said so) - this is that
; wiring. No dependencies; it includes no other library, so it can sit
; anywhere its consumers come after it.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4"
; I2C. The BCM2711 BSC (Broadcom Serial Control) master, wired in for the
; `i2c` command through the HwI2c* seam (RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_i2c.pi4 over
; this). It DEPENDS ON gpio.pi4 above - I2cInit() muxes GPIO 2/3 to ALT0
; (SDA1/SCL1) through gpio.pi4's PinAlt rather than re-deriving the
; out-of-order ALT table, so it must come AFTER gpio.pi4 (house rule 10: a
; library never includes a library; the order is written here). Its header
; carries the BSC register citations against BCM2711 ARM Peripherals ch.3.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/i2c.pi4"
; The Waveshare DSI panel's two I2C devices, both of which sit on the
; display connector's bus - BSC0 on GPIO 44/45 - and both of which come
; AFTER i2c.pi4 (house rule 10: a library never includes a library; the
; order is written here). touch_goodix.pi4 is the Goodix GT9xx touch
; controller, and it is the reason i2c.pi4 grew I2cWriteRead(): its
; register pointer is sixteen bits and does not survive the STOP that the
; two-transaction read path puts in the middle.
;
; WHICH PANEL MCU, AND WHY IT IS THE v2 ONE. Waveshare ship two
; incompatible generations that both answer at $45. The panel on this
; bench is the v2 board - the overlay line for it is
; vc4-kms-dsi-waveshare-panel-v2,10_1_inch_a - whose MCU is a 16-bit GPIO
; EXPANDER plus a PWM at registers $94..$99, so dsi_panel_v2.pi4 is what
; is compiled in. Lib/dsi_panel.pi4, the v1 register file, is NOT: its
; $AB and $AD are outside the v2 MCU's regmap and an out-of-range write
; to a microcontroller is undocumented behaviour, not a no-op. The v1
; library stays in the tree for a v1 board and is exercised by the desk
; gate through Examples/Diagnostics/pi4TouchProbe.pi4.
;
; dsi_panel_v2.pi4 ALSO OWNS THE TOUCH CONTROLLER'S RESET. It is expander
; bit 9 and no Pi GPIO reaches it, so the Goodix cannot answer until the
; MCU has been written - which is why the `touch` command below powers
; the MCU before it probes the touch address, and says so when it does.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dsi_panel_v2.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/touch_goodix.pi4"
; The DSI1 HOST - the SoC's side of the ribbon. A different part from the
; MCU above, reached over a different bus, and the two prove nothing
; about each other: the MCU answered on 2026-09-06 with the glass still
; dark, because nothing had ever been said to the panel itself.
;
; dsi_host.pi4 needs only timer.pi4. dsi_panel_v2_dcs.pi4 needs BOTH
; lines above it AND dsi_host.pi4, because the panel's rails are switched
; over I2C while its 200-command init table goes out over DSI.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dsi_host.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dsi_panel_v2_dcs.pi4"
; PWM, the SoC temperature, and the fan curve - the three halves of the
; `fan` command, in dependency order.
;
; Lib/pwm.pi4 DEPENDS ON gpio.pi4 above (it muxes the pin through PinAlt
; and drives the software channel through PinHigh/PinLow) and on
; timer.pi4 (Micros() is the software channel's clock and the clock
; manager's busy timeout). Both are already included further up.
;
; Lib/thermal.pi4 DEPENDS ON mailbox.pi4 above - the fallback reading and
; the throttle temperature are firmware property tags. Its preferred path
; is a plain register read and needs nothing.
;
; THE CURVE ITSELF IS NOT HERE. It is Anvil/Core/fan.pbi, included with
; the other core files further down, because a ramp between two
; temperatures is not a fact about a BCM2711 and the core command that
; uses it is compiled for every board. Its own header carries the
; reasoning. House rule 10 applies to the two below - neither includes
; the other, so the order is stated here.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/pwm.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/thermal.pi4"
; The MMU and caches. Compiled in for the runtime `cache` command; the
; caches are OFF at boot and turned on only by hand, so the resident
; monitor still enters the prompt with the stub's $30C50830 every time
; and a bad enable is recovered by a power cycle, never a brick. See
; CacheEnable/CacheDisable below and CmdCache.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4"
; The Hardware Video Scaler and pixel valve 1 - the path from a
; framebuffer in DRAM to the DSI host. It DEPENDS ON clocks.pi4 above,
; because the HVS runs on the VideoCore core clock and that clock is the
; firmware's to hand out, not a CPRMAN register. It does not depend on
; display.pi4 and is not part of the firmware framebuffer path: the
; firmware owns no display on this board and the two never meet.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/hvs.pi4"
; #DSP_DMA_LINKED - MANDATORY, and it must be defined BEFORE display.pi4.
; 0 = the CPU drawing path and nothing else; every procedure that would
; touch the blitter is skipped at the token level and never parsed.
; 1 additionally requires RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4 to be included AHEAD
; of display.pi4, which is why dma.pi4 is on the line above and not down
; with the storage libraries: definition order is the convention in this
; dialect and display.pi4 names DmaCopy, DmaCopy2D, DmaFillPattern32,
; DmaCacheRange and DmaSetBounds.
; display.pi4's THE DMA SEAM section has the reasoning; there is no
; default because a default in the library and a different value here
; would be a hard "Constant already defined" error.
;
; ======================================================================
;  IT WAS 0 UNTIL 2026-08-28, AND THE REPORT THAT CHANGED IT WAS
;  "THERE IS NO ACCELERATION IN THE SCROLLING". THERE WAS NOT. THIS
;  LINE IS WHY.
; ======================================================================
;  Measured on this board that day by
;  RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4ScrollTime.pi4 - 1920 x 1080 at
;  32bpp, pitch 7680, a 240 x 60 console under the banner, MMU off,
;  which is exactly this program's configuration - ONE scroll of the
;  default eight-row chunk:
;
;      CPU, this monitor as it stood              527.9 ms
;      the same scroll through the engine         213.9 ms
;      the engine's two transfers, alone           12.0 ms
;
;  Half a second, per scroll, on a console that scrolls once every
;  eight printed lines. The board was not busy and the bus was not
;  slow: a full-surface CPU clear of 8.29 MB measures 355.4 ms here,
;  which agrees with the 356.9 ms dma.pi4's header already recorded, so
;  the harness is believable and the number is the loop.
;
;  THE ENGINE IS NOT THE WHOLE STORY AND THIS COMMENT WILL NOT PRETEND
;  IT IS. 213.9 of the 214 ms above is cache maintenance, not transfer -
;  see THE SCROLL, AND WHY IT IS PRE-EMPTED at ScreenFastScroll() below
;  for the measurement and for the one library defect that is left.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4"
#DSP_DMA_LINKED = 1
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE V3D / NeonArcade CONSOLE, behind #ANVIL_V3D_CONSOLE.
;
;  A second console backend that renders the HDMI text with the GPU
;  instead of the DMA/CPU text grid, double-buffered with a vertical-blank
;  page flip. It is OPT-IN at run time (`screen v3d`, back with `screen
;  dma`) and the DMA console stays the default and the fallback, so a board
;  with no V3D - or one where V3dInit fails - is unaffected. Set the flag to
;  0 to compile the whole thing out (the three libraries below are then dead
;  code and pruned, and the DMA console is all there is). The engine and its
;  QPU assembler are the same three libraries the pi4Neon* diagnostics use.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ANVIL_V3D_CONSOLE = 1
; The screen/cache policy reads this build choice before cache.pi4 is included.
#ANVIL_VULKAN_CONSOLE = 1

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE FAN IN THE BANNER, behind #ANVIL_BANNER_FAN.
;
;  The banner's identity row carries the part's temperature beside the
;  clock on every board - every Pi 4 has the sensor. The fan's duty rides
;  beside it only when this constant is 1 AND a fan pin is actually being
;  driven (`fan pin`), so a board with no fan wired shows nothing about a
;  fan. Most boards have no PWM fan; a board file that never will can set
;  this to 0 and the fan half is not even compiled. Project ruling,
;  2026-09-11: the duty is wanted in the banner where one is being
;  driven, and most of these boards will never have a modulated fan, so
;  the fan half is behind a switch rather than behind a run-time test
;  alone.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ANVIL_BANNER_FAN = 1
CompilerIf #ANVIL_V3D_CONSOLE = 1
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/v3dqpu.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/v3d.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/neon.pi4"
CompilerEndIf
; STORAGE, for the f command. emmc.pi4 gives block-level SD access and
; fat.pi4 parses a filesystem on top of it - fat.pi4 deliberately does
; NOT include emmc.pi4 itself; it takes its block reader by pointer, so
; a USB mass-storage reader can replace the card later without it
; changing. That indirection is why the FAT parser could be tested
; against a fabricated volume with no card in the machine.
; The USB path to a block device. Order matters: xhci.pi4 uses pcie.pi4
; and usbmsc.pi4 uses xhci.pi4, and a library never includes a library,
; so the main file lists them in dependency order.
; THE USB PROTOCOL IS NOT THIS CHIP'S. Descriptor fetches, the walk over a
; configuration descriptor, string descriptors and the class names are the
; same over an xHCI controller here and a DWC2 on a Pi 3, so they live in
; one shared driver and every board reaches them (rule 30). It carries no
; hardware knowledge at all - the board hands it two control-transfer
; procedures and nothing else - and it is listed FIRST because the
; controller driver and the mass-storage driver above both use it.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Bus/usb_core.pbi"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/pcie.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/xhci.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/usbmsc.pi4"
; The mouse cursor on the screen. hid.pi4 rides on the xhci.pi4 already
; linked above for the boot stick, so this costs one library and no new
; hardware bring-up.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/hid.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/emmc.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/fat.pi4"
; THE HAL FOUNDATION - the capability gate (RequireCap) and the portable
; vocabularies the Hw* seams speak back to the core (#HW_GPIO_*, #HW_I2C_*,
; #HW_USB_*, #HW_FILE_*). Core, chip-free, shared by every board. It sits
; here rather than beside the #CAP_* block below because the storage
; backend on the next line needs its #HW_FILE_* codes; it needs nothing
; itself but uart.pi4, far above, and it still precedes every gated
; command handler, which is the ordering rule that actually matters.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi"
; THE PI 4'S FILE-LEVEL STORAGE BACKEND - the HwFile* seam Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi
; declares, implemented over fat.pi4 just above. It is HERE, high up and
; well before Board/storage.pi4, for a mechanical reason its own header
; sets out: the settings store on the next line calls HwFile*, so the
; backend must precede it - while the medium BRING-UP cannot, because
; StorageUp() calls UsbEnumerate() in Board/cursor_input.pi4, which needs
; the display. The bring-up is therefore split off as HwStorageUp() at the
; bottom of Board/storage.pi4, and only the file operations live here.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Storage/hwfile.pbi"
; settings.pi4 is the KEY=VALUE store behind the `settings`, `wifi` and
; `boot` commands. It reaches the medium ONLY through the HwFile* seam
; above - which is why it lives in Anvil/Core and compiles unchanged for
; the Arduino UNO Q, whose files come from UEFI and not from a FAT of
; ours. It still never installs a block writer itself; hwfile.pbi arms
; one around the single call that writes and disarms it on every path out.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/settings.pbi"
; THE NETWORK, for get, put and net. Three libraries, none of which does
; a byte of input or output on its own - see THE NETWORK further down for
; what that costs this file and why it is worth it. The order is the one
; the dependency runs in even though none of them includes another: the
; MAC moves frames, net.pi4 puts IPv4 and UDP inside them, and tftp.pi4
; puts TFTP inside that. A library never includes a library, so the order
; here is the only place it is written down.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/genet.pi4"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Network/net.pbi"
; ONE ADDRESS RECORD PER INTERFACE, and the one IP layer pointed at
; whichever interface the frame in hand concerns. Every address this
; board binds goes through it - see its header for the measurement that
; made it necessary.
;
; STRAIGHT AFTER net.pi4, AND THE POSITION IS LOAD-BEARING. It declares
; the #NET_ADDR_* codes, and RaspberryPi4/Lib/wifi.pi4 - compiled below -
; names one of them when it binds the radio's own boot lease; a constant
; may not be USED before it is DECLARED. Every PROCEDURE it calls
; (HwLink*, LinkPin) is resolved forward, which is what lets it sit
; above the two files that supply them.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/netif.pbi"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/tftp.pi4"
; dns.pi4 and dhcp.pi4 are two more payload codecs riding net.pi4's UDP,
; the same way tftp.pi4 does - they build and parse the DNS and DHCP
; messages and do no I/O. They come after net.pi4 because they use its
; byte-order helpers, and before net_cmd.pi4, which runs their pump.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dns.pi4"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Network/dhcp.pbi"
; THE DHCP SERVER, for a cable where nothing answered this board's own
; discovers. After netif.pi4, because it takes the served address as an
; interface ALIAS beside the link-local one; after dhcp.pi4, whose
; constants and message layout it shares with the client so the two
; halves of one protocol cannot drift apart. The console's pump feeds it.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/dhcpd.pbi"
; THE RADIO. sdio.pi4 and cyw43.pi4 are the CYW43455 driver; wifi.pi4
; is the monitor-side flow over them - read the firmware off the
; stick, bring the radio up, scan, and match what is on the air
; against the networks stored in the settings table.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/sdio.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43_rx_glom.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43.pi4"
; The WPA2 host supplicant and the crypto it needs. The 43455 has no
; firmware supplicant, so the four-way handshake runs here.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/sha1.pi4"
; sha256.pi4 backs the sha256sum command (Anvil/Core/hash_cmd.pbi). It was
; already in the tree, verified, but nothing built it in until now.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/sha256.pbi"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/hmacsha1.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/pbkdf2.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/aes.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/keywrap.pi4"
; hmac, hkdf, drbg and gcm - the TLS-side crypto. Every one of them was
; written and gated in the MODEL (tools/a64/a64_gcm_check.py and the A64
; cross gates in tools/check_tls_twins.py), and not one had ever been
; built into a board image, so not one instruction of them had ever run
; on the part. The `crypto` self-test below is what executes them, on
; the same vectors, which is why they are here now.
;
; gcm.pi4's include was deliberately left out on the day it landed, on
; the correct grounds that an unreachable module in a boot image is an
; unrequested feature. It has a caller now.
;
; The ORDER here is the one drbg.pi4's own header states - sha256 (far
; above), then hmac, then entropy, then drbg - because a library in this
; tree names its dependencies in its header and does not include them
; itself (house rule 10).
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/hmac.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/hkdf.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/gcm.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/entropy.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/drbg.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/wpa2sup.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/wifi.pi4"
; THE LINK SEAM AND TCP. hw_link.pi4 is the BOARD's side of the HwLink*
; seam and must come after BOTH drivers, because it is now the only file
; in this build that names them; link.pi4 is portable policy over that
; seam and must come after hw_link.pi4 and after net.pi4; tcp.pi4 must
; come after link.pi4. http.pi4 is further down, after
; Anvil/Core/rxbreak.pbi, because it asks the monitor for
; NetBreakCheck().
;
; UNTIL 2026-09-04 link.pi4 named genet, cyw43 and wifi itself, and its
; own header said a board without a radio could not include it. That one
; sentence was what kept the whole portable stack above it - net, ping,
; dns, dhcp, tcp, http - off every other board. See Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi
; under HwLink*.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_link.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/link.pi4"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/netfmt.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/sntp_codec.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/ntp_service.pbi"
; THE NETWORK CONSOLE - one console, on EVERY interface that holds an
; address. It was inside wifi.pi4 until 2026-09-07 and was called the
; wireless console; it named the radio in every line, so a board reachable
; only over the Ethernet cable had no console at all. It is portable core
; now: every wire access in it is HwLink*(kind, ...), so it comes after
; hw_link.pi4, after net.pi4 and after uart.pi4, and it is the only file
; that decides where a console listens.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/netconsole.pbi"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/tcp.pi4"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Applications/Forum/http_head.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Services/Http/transport_tcp.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Services/Http/server.pbi"
; The `net recv` RECEIVER - the state machine only, no console and no
; parser. It goes here, immediately after tcp.pi4, because those two and
; Sha256Of (Core/sha256.pi4, above) and millis() (Lib/timer.pi4, above)
; are the whole of what it calls. Keeping its dependency list that short
; is what lets tools/a64/a64_tcp_check.py compile it into the frame-exact
; TCP harness and drive it with a scripted peer; its console half is
; Anvil/Core/netrecv_cmd.pbi, much further down beside the other
; commands.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Core/netrecv.pbi"

; The banner anvil, as pixels. Generated by tools/gen_anvil_pic.py from
; anvil_logo.png; #ANVIL_PIC_W/H and the anvilPix DataSection live here.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil_logo.pi4"

